  "id": "DrugBank_DB13762 - MONDO_4247",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Dexrabeprazole may be associated with Peptic Ulcer Disease through a series of pharmacological pathways. The drug is indicated for Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome, a condition characterized by excessive gastric acid production, and it is also indicated for Hyperinsulinism, a disorder involving abnormal insulin secretion. Both of these conditions are linked to Peptic Ulcer Disease, which is primarily caused by excessive acid secretion. Additionally, Dexrabeprazole is indicated for Duodenal Ulcer (disease), and Nizatidine, a proton pump inhibitor, is indicated for Peptic Ulcer Disease. The cumulative evidence indicates that Dexrabeprazole may exert a protective effect against peptic ulcers by reducing acid secretion, which is a common mechanism underlying the development of Peptic Ulcer Disease. The scores from the different paths suggest a moderate level of support for this relationship, though the strength varies across the pathways. The evidence is consistent across multiple indications, reinforcing the potential for Dexrabeprazole to be repurposed for the treatment of Peptic Ulcer Disease.",
